title: 'Forking: seeing your version where the original would appear'
pid: 67b25333-c250-4e98-99d4-09d4da3bc0eb
body: |2-
  When you clone a foreign node, the clone =forks= the original. The clone is the /forker/, the original the /forkee/, and the two are /mirrors/ of each other. That changes what gets shown: wherever the original would appear as recursive content of some other node, Skg shows the mirror the view's perspective ranks first in its place. This is /mirror substitution/. So once you have cloned =mystical= into your skgrepo, every view of a note of that skgrepo that would have shown Egg Man's =mystical= as content shows your =eggs / mystical= instead. A view rooted at a node of Egg Man's is drawn from his skgrepo: his own forks, not yours (below).

  Substitution is careful in four ways:

  - *It round-trips to the original on save.* The shown fork carries an =inPlaceOf= part (shown as pink =ĥ= after =K=; see [[id:ef1f3f60-2e51-4584-8e5d-7dd6f6214cd2][the herald legend]]) naming the node it stands for. When you save the container, Skg records the /original/'s ID in the container's content list, not the fork's, so showing your version never silently rewrites someone's contains list. Under a container that is not yours, deleting the shown fork deletes it and leaves the original in the list, so that container is not forked, and the original is drawn there again; under one of yours, the deletion removes the original from the list too, as removing the fork's headline does anywhere ([[id:1c6e493f-b9ff-49c2-8642-729de8b37e85][forks.org]], "Taking back a fork").
  - *It is one level deep.* A node that is itself shown raw — a member of a folder other than a member as such of a forks folder, or a view root whose perspective would show another mirror — shows its own children raw. You are looking at the original there, so its children are the original's. Substitution resumes one level further down.
  - *A view follows one skgrepo's perspective: its root's home.* A view's perspective is a skgrepo, by default the home skgrepo of each tree's root. From a skgrepo, a note shows its mirror in that skgrepo (the note itself, if it is homed there), else its mirror in the skgrepo the config declares first (=docs/mirrors.org=, "Which mirror stands"). So under a root of your own, your fork in that root's skgrepo stands in its original's place, and if someone else's node forks one of yours, yours still stands; their fork is a display-only fact there (it can earn an herald). Under a root of someone else's, their skgrepo ranks the mirrors, throughout the view, even beneath a third party's notes. So a node you fork in such a view is shown there, after the save, as the mirror their skgrepo ranks first, usually the original; your clone is shown from your clone's skgrepo. To see your version in context, switch the view to that skgrepo (=C-c v o= in Emacs, =<localleader>vo= in Neovim, which cycles through every skgrepo whose mirrors would show the view differently, then back), view your clone of the root, or edit the root, which forks it and makes the clone the view's root. A private and a public fork of one note can each be chosen so.
  - *Visibility gates substitution.* A fork whose skgrepo the current skgrepo restriction keeps out cannot be shown, so it does not substitute; the original is shown instead. Switching skgrepo-sets can therefore change which node a view shows.

  Any number of notes, owned or not, may fork one node, and forks may form chains and cycles: you may fork your own fork, so =D forks C forks N= (all owned) is fine, and so are two of your notes forking one node, or two forking each other. Which of them a view shows in a node's place is mirror substitution's choice (=docs/mirrors.org=, "Which mirror stands"), which has no cycle case. The motivation for chains is layered forks — clone a foreign node into a public clone =C=, then fork =C= again into a private =D= that forks it (see =docs/forks.org=, the explicit fork gesture). What an owned note may not do, checked both when you save and when the databases are rebuilt, is fork itself, contain or hide itself, or contain a note it forks or that forks it (=docs/data-model_technical.org=, "Hard validation constraints").

  The node Skg shows for =N= is the mirror of =N= (=N= or a note joined to it by =forks= relationships, either way) that the perspective's list of skgrepos ranks first; in its own skgrepo =N= itself stands, and within one skgrepo other than =N='s, a chain shows its end (=docs/mirrors.org=, "Mirror substitution"). Under a skgrepo restriction that keeps out the chain's end, the shown node is a /middle/ of the chain; its =(inPlaceOf N)= part is still honest, as any mirror's is, and the save still round-trips =N= into the container's contains.

  When you ask for a brand-new content view of a node, Skg opens the requested node itself: drawn from its own skgrepo, it stands as itself. It does not show one of the root's mirrors in its place, until you switch the view to another skgrepo's perspective (=C-c v o=), which shows that skgrepo's mirror of the root, as of every other node. Fork facts remain visible in the heralds (the =K= token); automatic substitution applies to recursive content, and to the root once the perspective is another skgrepo's.
